# Per-test plusargs for exc_priority_irq_vs_sync.
#
# This test exercises sync-exception (TRAP #0) vs IRQ priority arbitration.
# The test asm itself cannot raise an external IPL — that's a peripheral
# action.  Inject IPL=1 from the testbench at cycle 250 (chosen so the
# TRAP handler has had time to retire its `addq.l #1, COUNTER` and
# `move.l #1, ORDER` stores before the IRQ takes the boundary on the
# way back to mainline).  The handler order check then validates that
# the TRAP ran first (ORDER==1 when IRQ checks) and the IRQ ran second
# (final ORDER==2, COUNTER==2).
#
# +ipl=cycle:level — single-shot IPL pulse.  Cleared on cpu_ipl_ack
# (i.e. when the CPU dispatches the IRQ exception, the irq_agg-style
# acknowledge pulse from commit.v drops cur_ipl_ext back to 0).
#
# Cycle bumped 250 → 280 (2026-05-06): the I-side MMU translator
# (rtl/core/fetch/if_mmu_xlate.v) added 2-3 cycles of latency to
# each fetch handshake.  The original 250 was tuned for the pre-
# walker fetch path and now lands before the TRAP handler retires
# its ORDER=1 store.  280 puts the IPL inject just after that
# store retires under the new latency.
+ipl=280:1
